-- Passage rg5-comparing-texts-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('eeba8748-d89a-5e65-b50e-75779ef3a434', 'rg5-comparing-texts-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g5-comparing-texts'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Learning the Hard Way$q$, $q$Text 1: Letters Backward (historical fiction)

Long ago, Samuel worked as a helper in a print shop. His job was to set small metal letters into a frame, one by one, to build each line of a page. The hard part was that every letter had to be placed backward.

On his first try, Samuel spelled "cat" as "tac." Mr. Hollis, the printer, only chuckled. "Every printer starts that way," he said.

Samuel practiced each night after the shop closed. His fingers grew stained with ink. After three months, he set a whole page of the town newspaper by himself. Mr. Hollis checked it and found no mistakes at all. Samuel grinned so wide his cheeks hurt.

Text 2: Tap, Tap, Tap (historical fiction)

In 1880, Clara lived above the train station where her father ran the telegraph. Messages came in as short and long clicks, and he wrote them down as words. Clara wanted to learn, but the clicks went too fast for her.

So she practiced. She tapped codes on the kitchen table with a spoon. She tapped them on her desk at school until her teacher frowned.

One winter evening, her father lay in bed with a fever. The telegraph began to click. Clara grabbed a pencil and wrote every word. The message said a snowstorm had stopped the evening train. Clara ran to tell the families on the platform, and they went home to stay warm.$q$, 'draft')
